# 6. IMPORTANT BUSINESS-RULE INTERPRETATIONS

Implement these carefully and document them.

## Price boundary

- Price at or below maximum: PASS.
- Price above maximum but no more than 3%: HUMAN REVIEW.
- Price more than 3% above maximum: REJECT.
- Exactly 3% above maximum: HUMAN REVIEW.

Do not round before comparison.

## Quantity boundary

- Deviation of exactly 5%: no quantity-review trigger.
- Deviation greater than 5%: HUMAN REVIEW.

## Delivery boundary

- 30 days or less: no delivery-review trigger.
- 31–45 days: HUMAN REVIEW.
- More than 45 days: REJECT.
- Exactly 45 days: HUMAN REVIEW.
- Exactly 46 days: REJECT.

## Advance payment boundary

- 20%: PASS.
- More than 20%: REJECT.

## Payment terms

Validate the advance and remaining payment separately.

Examples:

- 15% advance and remaining 45 days after delivery: PASS.
- 25% advance: REJECT.
- 10% advance and remaining 75 days after delivery: HUMAN REVIEW.
- Payment deadline unclear: HUMAN REVIEW.

Do not assume that wording such as NET 30 or NET 45 is compliant without establishing the reference event.

## Late-delivery penalty

Verify:

- The clause exists.
- The rate is at least 0.5%.
- The penalty is based on order value.
- The period is one week of delay.
- The clause actually applies to late delivery.

If any of these are ambiguous, request human review.

## VAT

Verify the VAT rate, VAT amount, and separate presentation.

VAT must be separately identified at 15%.

## Financial rounding

Use Python Decimal instead of float.

Define an explicit currency-rounding tolerance, preferably BDT 0.01 where appropriate.

Do not silently accept unexplained discrepancies.

